<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Ke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/367">367</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Ke was a prince and general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a>. In 352 he defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a>, ruler of Ran Wei, at Liantai, opening the way to Ye. As regent for the young emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a> from 360 he was a capable administrator and commander, and his death in 367 left the court to rivalries that weakened the state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murong Ke was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a>, by his concubine Consort Gao, and his father at first thought little of him because he did not favour his mother. The Book of Jin describes him as over 2.1 metres tall. Before 345 he held the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> against Goguryeo, which he attacked successfully in 345, and in 346 he took the capital of Buyeo and its king, Hyeon of Buyeo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the Later Zhao state collapsed, Murong Ke was among the main generals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. His army was said to be so disciplined that it harmed not even trees and grass. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a>'s infantry he feigned repeated defeats to draw the enemy into the plains, then broke them with cavalry chained in square formations. After the fall of Yecheng late in 352, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> claimed the imperial title and made him Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> in 353.</p><p>In the following years he subdued former Later Zhao generals, forced the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-kan">Duan Kan</a> at Guanggu in 356 and took Jin commanderies south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> in 358. Around the new year of 360 the dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> offered him the throne; Murong Ke declined and became regent for the ten-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a>.</p><p>As regent he executed the senior official Muyu Gen, who had accused him of plotting rebellion, and consulted his uncle Murong Ping on all important decisions. Sima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a>'s Zizhi Tongjian credits him with ruling his soldiers through kindness rather than punishment, and no recorded battle of his ended in defeat. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> from Jin in 365.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Falling ill in 367, he urged that command of the armies go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, whose abilities he admired. After his death Murong Ping took most of the power and gave the post instead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chong">Murong Chong</a>. Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> in 370, three years later. The Eastern Wei general Murong Shaozong was among his descendants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong Ke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The regent <b>Murong Ke</b> was a capable administrator and general.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 352 the Former Yan general <b>Murong Ke</b> defeated and captured Ran Min at Liantai; he was executed, and Ye fell later that year.</blockquote><a href="/state/ran-wei">Ran Wei</a></li></ul></section>
